      "payload": {
        "lesson": {
          "id": "orbitals",
          "label": "Atomic orbitals and quantum numbers",
          "title": "Atomic orbitals and quantum numbers",
          "molecule": "ethene",
          "tag": "Orbitals describe probability, not paths",
          "cards": {
            "rule": "<p>n = 1,2,… sets the shell. l = 0,…,n−1 sets the subshell (s,p,d,f). ml = −l,…,+l labels orbitals; ms = ±1/2 labels electron spin. Each orbital holds at most two electrons with opposite spins.</p>",
            "cue": "<p>One s orbital; three p orbitals; five d orbitals. A p subshell can hold six electrons.</p>",
            "trap": "<p>Orbital phase signs are wavefunction signs, not positive and negative electric charges. Electrons do not orbit like planets.</p>",
            "app": "<p>Carbon bonding uses valence orbitals from the second shell. Hybridization is a model for bonding and geometry, not a change in the allowed quantum numbers.</p>"
          },
          "steps": [
            {
              "label": "1. Count valence electrons",
              "text": "<p>An isolated neutral carbon atom has configuration 1s²2s²2p². Its four valence electrons occupy the n=2 shell. In molecules, orbitals combine and mix to describe bonding.</p>",
              "molecule": "ethene",
              "highlights": {
                "prin": [
                  0
                ]
              },
              "reveal": false
            },
            {
              "label": "2. Fill degenerate orbitals",
              "text": "<p>Hund’s rule places electrons singly with parallel spins in degenerate orbitals before pairing. Pauli exclusion forbids two electrons in an atom from sharing all four quantum numbers.</p>",
              "molecule": "ethene",
              "highlights": {
                "prin": [
                  0,
                  1
                ]
              },
              "reveal": false
            }
          ]
