      "payload": {
        "lesson": {
          "id": "sigma-pi",
          "label": "Sigma, pi, and molecular orbitals",
          "title": "Sigma, pi, and molecular orbitals",
          "molecule": "ethene",
          "tag": "End-on vs. side-on overlap",
          "cards": {
            "rule": "<p>A sigma bond has electron density along the internuclear axis. A pi bond comes from side-on p-orbital overlap. A double bond has one sigma and one pi; a triple bond has one sigma and two pi. Constructive overlap forms a lower-energy bonding MO; destructive overlap gives an antibonding MO with an added node.</p>",
            "cue": "<p>Bond order = (bonding electrons − antibonding electrons)/2 in the simple MO model. More bond order usually means shorter, stronger bonds for a given atom pair.</p>",
            "trap": "<p>A pi bond restricts rotation because twisting disrupts overlap. Bond rotation is possible only by paying an energy cost; do not imagine a double bond freely rotating at room temperature.</p>",
            "app": "<p>Carbonyl addition converts a pi bond into sigma bonds. Following sigma and pi electrons helps explain the change from planar to tetrahedral geometry.</p>"
          },
          "steps": [
            {
              "label": "1. Double bond inventory",
              "text": "<p>Ethene has one C–C sigma bond, one C–C pi bond, and four C–H sigma bonds: five sigma and one pi overall.</p>",
              "molecule": "ethene",
              "highlights": {
                "prin": [
                  0,
                  1
                ]
              },
              "reveal": true
            },
            {
              "label": "2. Triple bond inventory",
              "text": "<p>Ethyne has one C–C sigma plus two perpendicular pi bonds. Add two C–H sigma bonds: three sigma and two pi overall.</p>",
              "molecule": "ethyne",
              "highlights": {
