          "label": "Hybridization, geometry, and resonance",
          "title": "Hybridization, geometry, and resonance",
          "molecule": "acetamide",
          "tag": "Count domains, then check conjugation",
          "cards": {
            "rule": "<p>Typical tetrahedral carbon is sp³ (about 109.5°), trigonal planar carbon is sp² (about 120°), and linear carbon is sp (180°). The s character is 25%, 33%, and 50%, respectively. Resonance contributors differ only in electron placement, not atom connectivity.</p>",
            "cue": "<p>For ordinary carbon: four electron domains → sp³; three → sp²; two → sp. A multiple bond counts as one domain.</p>",
            "trap": "<p>Do not mechanically label an amide nitrogen sp³ just because it has three sigma bonds and a lone pair. Its lone pair occupies a p orbital conjugated with the carbonyl, making the amide approximately planar.</p>",
            "app": "<p>Peptide-bond resonance restricts C–N rotation. Delocalized charge explains carboxylate stability and why its two C–O bonds are equivalent in the resonance hybrid.</p>"
          },
          "steps": [
            {
              "label": "1. Carbonyl geometry",
              "text": "<p>The carbonyl carbon has three sigma-bond directions and a p orbital for the pi bond: sp² and approximately planar. Its methyl neighbor is sp³.</p>",
